<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonardo Bruni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1370–<a href="/years/1444">1444</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonardo Bruni (c. 1370–1444) was an Italian humanist and historian who served as chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1410–1411 and again from 1427 until his death. A pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coluccio-salutati">Coluccio Salutati</a>, he translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> from Greek into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and praised Florentine republican liberty in his Panegyric to the City of Florence. His History of the Florentine People is often called the first modern work of history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni.jpg" alt="Leonardo Bruni" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walker_Art_Gallery,_Leonard_Bruni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
